[2025-09-23 20:59:14,167][Bodhi.src.Bodhi.parallel_kg_extractor][INFO] - Concise summary: 
This paper introduces ViCo, a method for learning word embeddings from visual co-occurrences extracted from large-scale, textually-annotated visual databases like VisualGenome and ImageNet. The core concept is to leverage multiple types of visual co-occurrences (e.g., object-hypernym, object-attribute) to create embeddings that capture visual concept similarities and differences better than text-only embeddings like GloVe.  ViCo is evaluated through unsupervised clustering, supervised partitioning, and zero-shot-like generalization analysis, demonstrating improved performance when combined with GloVe. Key datasets include VisualGenome, ImageNet, and WordNet, with annotations used to compute co-occurrence statistics. The paper also explores the application of ViCo in vision-language tasks like image retrieval, VQA, and captioning, showing superior results compared to GloVe.  The methodology involves a multi-task approach using a log-bilinear model and type-specific transformation functions.  The paper also compares ViCo to existing methods like vis-w2v and analyzes its performance in relation to GloVe and random embeddings.


